Running the Application
There are three ways to run FitPic depending on your use case.

Method 1 — Web Interface (Recommended)
This is the primary way to use FitPic. It requires two steps: starting the backend server, then opening the frontend.

Step 1 — Start the API server:
python main.py

You should see:
FITPIC API SERVER STARTING...
URL:  http://localhost:8000
Docs: http://localhost:8000/docs

Step 2 — Open the homepage:
Open homepage.html in your browser by double-clicking the file, or run:

# Mac
open homepage.html

# Windows
start homepage.html

The homepage will load the FitPic interface and communicate with the server running at http://localhost:8000 to fetch outfit recommendations.

Note: The terminal running python main.py must remain open the entire time. Closing it stops the server.

Method 2 — Command Line Interface
Runs FitPic entirely in the terminal with no browser or server required. Useful for testing the recommendation engine directly.
python model.py

The program will prompt you to enter preferences step by step:
What's your name?
What would you like to shop for?   (Women / Men / No preference)
What style are you going for?      (Casual, Streetwear, Formal, etc.)
What's the occasion?               (Everyday, Work, Date, etc.)
What's your skin tone?             (Fair, Medium, Deep, etc.)
What season are you dressing for?

At the end, your top 5 outfit recommendations are printed with match scores, colors, prices, and direct shopping links.

Method 3 — API Server Only
If you want to query the recommendation engine directly without a frontend (e.g. for testing or integration), start the server and use the interactive API docs.
python main.py

Then open in your browser:
http://localhost:8000/docs

This opens Swagger UI where you can test every endpoint manually.

API Reference
POST /recommend
Returns outfit recommendations based on user preferences.

Request Body (JSON):
{
  "style":     "casual",
  "occasion":  "everyday",
  "skin_tone": "medium",
  "gender":    "Women"
}

Response:
{
  "success": true,
  "style": "casual",
  "total_found": 5,
  "outfits": [...]
}

GET /recommend/quick
Same as POST /recommend but accepts parameters in the URL.
GET /recommend/quick?style=casual&skin_tone=medium&gender=Women

GET /styles
Returns all valid values for style, occasion, and skin_tone.

GET /health
Returns server status. Useful for confirming the server is running.

Accepted Parameter Values
style
casual · streetwear · business_casual · formal · athleisure · bohemian · minimalist

occasion
everyday · work · night_out · date · campus · workout · travel

skin_tone
fair · light · medium · medium_warm · tan · deep

gender
Women · Men · (omit for no preference)

Data Pipeline
Recommendations are resolved in the following order of priority:

1.	Real-Time Product Search API — Queries one item per outfit slot (top, bottom, shoes, accessory). Each result is validated with a live URL check before being returned.
2.	ASOS API — Used if fewer than 3 results are returned from step 1.
3.	Local CSV Fallback — styles.csv is used if both APIs fail or return insufficient results. Items are scored and ranked by color compatibility and gender match.

Configuration
The RapidAPI key is defined at the top of model.py:

RAPIDAPI_KEY = "your-key-here"

This key covers both the ASOS API (asos10.p.rapidapi.com) and the Real-Time Product Search API (real-time-product-search.p.rapidapi.com). Replace it if the key expires or is rotated.

Data Exploration
To inspect the local dataset independently:

python explore_data.py

Outputs a breakdown of usage categories, article types, and sample records from styles.csv.

Troubleshooting
Problem	Fix
ModuleNotFoundError	Run: pip install fastapi uvicorn pandas requests pydantic
Homepage shows no results	Confirm python main.py is running in the terminal
Address already in use	Another process is using port 8000 — restart terminal and retry
python: command not found	Python is not installed or not added to PATH
API returns empty outfits	Check internet connection; system will fall back to styles.csv automatically
